<!--
# Hello Cloudflare Containers

Deploys `examples/beetmania` to Cloudflare Containers: the SAME native `beet` binary
as `fargate`, run in a container that reads the site from R2 at request time
via `S3Store::r2` and boots the example's `serve` command (`exec_route`). Served
on `<name>.workers.dev` (no custom domain). Run `just beetmania assets` first, so
the files the site borrows and derives are in the directory it syncs. Toggling
between this and the wasm Worker (`cloudflare_workers.bsx`) is a small swap:

- block: `CloudflareContainerBlock` <-> `CloudflareWorkerBlock`
- build: a `<BeetBinaryBuild/>` (native binary baked into the image) vs none (the
  Worker IS `beet-cli` wasm, built by `worker-build` on every deploy)
- deploy: `<CloudflareContainerDeployAction/>` (+ wrangler) <-> `<CloudflareWorkerDeployAction/>`

The served content and the deploy/sync/destroy route shape are identical.

## Usage

    beet --entry=examples/infra/cloudflare_containers.bsx deploy
    beet --entry=examples/infra/cloudflare_containers.bsx sync
    beet --entry=examples/infra/cloudflare_containers.bsx destroy

Live deploy needs `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` and the account declared on the root
(`{CloudflareAccount("..")}`, a placeholder below), plus `wrangler` and a
container engine. The Workers Paid plan is required for Containers. No R2 pair
is held anywhere: the deploy DERIVES the S3 credentials the container reads the
site with from that token (a token's id is the access key id, the sha256 of its
value the secret) and uploads them as the Worker's own secrets, so an R2
credential with object write over the account is never a record of a document
the age key opens. The container holds one as wide as the token the deploy ran
with, which is honest for an example whose bucket holds nothing but published
site files; a container of its own takes a token scoped to one bucket's
objects, the way an `<R2BucketBlock/>`'s is.

The token a package's document holds is lowered from what its DEPLOYED entry declares
(`<CloudflareDeployCredential/>`), so it carries no account-level Workers group: a live run
here passes a wider one for the command (`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=.. beet --entry=..
deploy`). Each action's `CloudflareAccess` names the groups its calls need.
-->
<CliServer {CallOnReady}>
<Router {(RequestLogger, HelpHandler, CloudflareAccount("0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef"))}>
	<RequireCfg cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra"/>
	<Route path="deploy" {ExchangeSequence}>
		<CloudflareContainerBlock name="beet-hello-container" bucket="beet-hello-site" exec_route="serve"/>
		<!-- build the native binary, then the deploy bakes it into the image -->
		<BeetBinaryBuild features="aws"/>
		<CloudflareContainerDeployAction/>
		<!-- the wasm runtime the site's pages boot, built by this verb rather
		     than borrowed: the sync uploads it through the site's `wasm/` links -->
		<WasmRuntime cargo_package="beet-cli" bin="beet" features="web_ui" out="target/wasm/beet-ui.wasm"/>
		<CloudflareR2Sync local_dir="examples/beetmania" bucket="beet-hello-site"/>
		<!-- the Cloudflare rollout is instant, so tail briefly then exit -->
		<CloudflareWatch name="beet-hello-container" timeout="10s"/>
	</Route>
	<Route path="sync" {ExchangeSequence}>
		<!-- the wasm runtime the site's pages boot, built by this verb rather
		     than borrowed: the sync uploads it through the site's `wasm/` links -->
		<WasmRuntime cargo_package="beet-cli" bin="beet" features="web_ui" out="target/wasm/beet-ui.wasm"/>
		<CloudflareR2Sync local_dir="examples/beetmania" bucket="beet-hello-site"/>
	</Route>
	<Route path="destroy" {ExchangeSequence}>
		<CloudflareDestroy name="beet-hello-container" bucket="beet-hello-site"/>
	</Route>
</Router>
</CliServer>
